WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.917 --> 00:00:04.129
If the universe is as we think
it might be,

00:00:05.547 --> 00:00:10.135
a sort of closed system
surrounded by a horizon,

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:14.139
and that that horizon behaves roughly
like the horizon of a black hole.

00:00:14.723 --> 00:00:19.060
Any self-contained system, if it satisfies
any kind of version of the standard

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:22.147
laws of physics, entropy will increase

00:00:22.147 --> 00:00:25.150
until it comes to thermal equilibrium.

00:00:26.443 --> 00:00:29.446
Once it comes to thermal equilibrium, um...

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:32.323
not much happens.

00:00:32.323 --> 00:00:35.326
Thermal equilibrium is extremely boring.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.413
It really is a dead world.

00:00:39.664 --> 00:00:42.125
However, the classical story

00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:47.047
of the increase of entropy misses
part of the evolution.

00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:50.508
Quantum entanglement is still evolving,

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:52.635
things keep happening.

00:00:52.635 --> 00:00:56.139
We propose the second law of
quantum complexity in direct analogy

00:00:56.139 --> 00:00:57.891
to the second law of thermodynamics.

00:00:57.891 --> 00:01:00.518
This is a really provocative suggestion.

00:01:00.518 --> 00:01:04.189
It struck me as something that did not compute.

00:01:04.272 --> 00:01:06.107
They came to the conclusion...

00:01:06.107 --> 00:01:08.485
old Lenny used to be a good physicist,

00:01:08.485 --> 00:01:12.030
he got into this complexity story,
but he didn't know what complexity was.

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:18.745
The standard laws of physics are given

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:22.999
their most rigorous stress tests
inside the most exotic of cosmic locales:

00:01:23.291 --> 00:01:25.001
black holes.

00:01:25.001 --> 00:01:28.630
On top of the much buzzed about work
on singularities and event horizons

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:29.923
in the 20th century.

00:01:29.923 --> 00:01:33.218
A new enigma has appeared having to do
with how big black holes

00:01:33.218 --> 00:01:35.136
are on the inside.

00:01:35.136 --> 00:01:39.766
About a decade ago, Stanford physicist,
Leonard Susskind, found that the interior

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:44.229
spacetime of a black hole is so warped
that it can seemingly grow forever.

00:01:44.979 --> 00:01:47.565
This appears to conflict
with the same laws of physics

00:01:47.565 --> 00:01:50.443
that bring a glass of ice water
to thermal equilibrium.

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:54.405
Those laws say that entropy
should increase toward a maximum value,

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:56.491
at which point things in the universe,

00:01:56.491 --> 00:02:00.078
including black holes, should stop
changing in any meaningful way.

00:02:00.495 --> 00:02:03.164
A fate referred to as a "heat death."

00:02:03.164 --> 00:02:06.376
At this point, why would 
black holes continue to change?

00:02:06.876 --> 00:02:10.547
Susskind and his collaborators
set out to solve this paradox,

00:02:10.547 --> 00:02:13.967
and in doing so,
they stumbled upon an unlikely explanation

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:17.470
for the near infinite expansion
of a black hole's interior.

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:21.015
My friend, who was my friend
and a good friend, Feynman,

00:02:21.266 --> 00:02:24.644
he would close his eyes
and visualize a system.

00:02:25.061 --> 00:02:28.064
And then when
he had a visual picture of it,

00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:30.733
he would convert that to mathematics.

00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:32.902
I don't have the mind of a mathematician,

00:02:32.902 --> 00:02:34.821
I have the mind of an auto mechanic.

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:37.240
What works is what's practical,

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.160
but sometimes you need some mathematics.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.163
I learned about black holes,
and there was something

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:46.624
about black holes that had nothing to do
with complexity at that time.

00:02:47.041 --> 00:02:50.044
But there was a puzzling feature
that I didn't understand.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:51.504
We had these pictures,

00:02:51.504 --> 00:02:53.423
they were called Penrose Diagrams.

00:02:53.423 --> 00:02:56.759
It's a kind of map of the whole universe,
including a black hole.

00:02:58.469 --> 00:02:59.450
The black hole is over here.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:01.639
Time goes upward.

00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:05.351
The interior of the black hole
had this strange behavior.

00:03:05.476 --> 00:03:11.316
The interior of the black hole
came to a smallest size and then expanded.

00:03:11.399 --> 00:03:14.736
It bounced and it started to grow.

00:03:15.236 --> 00:03:17.655
And I didn't know what
that bouncing thing was.

00:03:17.655 --> 00:03:19.490
And I gave up,
I didn't know what to make out of it.

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.367
I would ask my friends,
what's growing here?

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:26.122
Black hole experts –
and they would....

00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:26.915
I don't know...

00:03:26.915 --> 00:03:29.459
it's just the volume of the black hole.

00:03:29.459 --> 00:03:31.085
Okay.

00:03:31.085 --> 00:03:33.421
Physicists
started to look towards entropy,

00:03:33.421 --> 00:03:36.424
a concept that has a long history
in science and engineering.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:40.637
Entropy was invented by people
during the Industrial Revolution

00:03:40.845 --> 00:03:43.723
who wished to understand
the maximum possible efficiency

00:03:43.723 --> 00:03:46.726
of steam engines
and other similar machines.

00:03:46.935 --> 00:03:50.021
And what they were interested
in was the entropy

00:03:50.021 --> 00:03:53.024
of the working gas in their engine.

00:03:53.149 --> 00:03:58.154
So this was both a important theoretical
understanding and also big business.

00:03:58.363 --> 00:04:02.951
That is the story, though, of entropy -
a statistical claim

00:04:02.951 --> 00:04:06.537
that this disorder of the system
always increases.

00:04:06.788 --> 00:04:09.082
But for Susskind, something didn't fit.

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:12.585
I knew that it wasn't entropy
because the entropy

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:15.880
of the black hole
comes to thermal equilibrium very quickly.

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:19.425
On the other hand,
you can look at the Penrose Diagram

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:25.056
and you could see that expansion
lasted for a very, very long time.

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:28.768
So if you start with black coffee and milk

00:04:28.893 --> 00:04:32.605
and mix them together, it's not going to
take very long before you, in fact,

00:04:32.605 --> 00:04:37.485
have at least, as far as the intermixing
is concerned, thermodynamic equilibrium.

00:04:37.652 --> 00:04:40.905
And in fact, it'll look at least
as far as thermodynamic is concerned,

00:04:41.114 --> 00:04:42.865
like the arrow of time has stopped,

00:04:42.865 --> 00:04:44.951
the thermodynamic arrow of time
has ceased.

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:47.537
The system is now in a heat
death situation.

00:04:47.537 --> 00:04:50.623
There is no longer
any ability to generate more entropy.

00:04:51.916 --> 00:04:52.959
However, that is

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:57.255
not the end of the story
because, even though the system has reached

00:04:57.547 --> 00:05:02.260
thermodynamic equilibrium,
it is not reached complexity equilibrium.

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:03.678
Complex systems

00:05:03.678 --> 00:05:08.016
are characterized by individual parts
that interact in a multiplicity of ways,

00:05:08.182 --> 00:05:12.437
leading to phenomena like non-linearity, randomness, and emergence.

00:05:13.396 --> 00:05:15.481
Sensing that complexity might be at the root

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:19.610
of the explanation for why black holes
continue to grow past thermal equilibrium,

00:05:19.777 --> 00:05:21.612
Susskind and his collaborators turned

00:05:21.612 --> 00:05:24.615
to the mathematics
of theoretical computer science.

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:27.910
Feynman taught us
that the number of quantum

00:05:27.910 --> 00:05:32.040
states of a system is enormously large.

00:05:32.623 --> 00:05:34.662
If you have a certain number of qubits,

00:05:34.662 --> 00:05:40.673
the number of orthogonal quantum states of that system
is exponential in the number of qubits.

00:05:40.715 --> 00:05:45.219
What that means, is that for example,
a quantum computer or just an ordinary

00:05:45.219 --> 00:05:49.015
system will take an enormously long time

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:52.477
to explore the entire space of states.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:55.188
It will start out at some simple state

00:05:55.188 --> 00:05:58.191
and start
wandering around in the space of states

00:05:58.566 --> 00:06:03.112
much, much longer time than it takes
to establish thermal equilibrium.

00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:04.989
All of a sudden it would be bang -

00:06:04.989 --> 00:06:09.285
the complexity of a quantum state just
increases and increases and increases,

00:06:09.327 --> 00:06:12.288
long, long past the time

00:06:12.288 --> 00:06:15.583
when the system
has apparently come to thermal equilibrium

00:06:16.292 --> 00:06:20.546
And that this size of the interior
of the black hole was a direct measure

00:06:21.172 --> 00:06:24.008
of the complexity
of the quantum state of the black hole.

00:06:24.008 --> 00:06:27.970
When that hit me, I suddenly got really,

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:31.599
really interested in the concept
of computational complexity.

00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:40.066
So a classical system, we basically describe it like,

00:06:40.358 --> 00:06:43.861
Oh, I have a bunch of bits
and they can be 0101, right?

00:06:44.028 --> 00:06:48.199
I can just describe them individually
what state they are.

00:06:48.241 --> 00:06:50.326
But for a quantum system,
that's not enough.

00:06:50.326 --> 00:06:55.206
We, we cannot describe the system
by starting from the first qubit

00:06:55.206 --> 00:06:59.293
and then describe the second cubit
because they can be globally entangled

00:06:59.502 --> 00:07:03.631
and locally, there might not be information
at all, but globally

00:07:03.673 --> 00:07:06.676
they might be in some highly
non-trivial quantum state.

00:07:06.676 --> 00:07:11.055
Entanglement is the key to
why complexity can grow

00:07:11.055 --> 00:07:14.016
so large for a quantum system.

00:07:14.225 --> 00:07:18.229
Working with his Stanford colleague
Adam Brown, Susskind developed the idea

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:21.232
that even after
classical entropy has reached its maximum,

00:07:21.357 --> 00:07:24.193
the interior of a black hole
can continue to evolve,

00:07:24.193 --> 00:07:27.530
because of the ever increasing complexity
of its quantum state.

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:31.534
Meaning that there's life after heat,
death for black holes after all.

00:07:32.618 --> 00:07:35.204
And to make
their case to the scientific community,

00:07:35.204 --> 00:07:37.518
they borrowed a longstanding concept from computer
science

00:07:37.518 --> 00:07:39.292
known as Circuit Complexity.

00:07:39.459 --> 00:07:43.588
Originally formulated to measure the
complexity of a computer program's output.

00:07:43.963 --> 00:07:47.675
Quantum circuit complexity had become
the unlikely mathematical language

00:07:47.675 --> 00:07:52.054
for a new theory attempting to explain
the evolution of black holes, a theory

00:07:52.555 --> 00:07:55.558
that wasn't without its critics.

00:07:56.767 --> 00:07:59.103
I first heard that there was a connection

00:07:59.103 --> 00:08:02.106
being drawn between circuit complexity
and black holes

00:08:03.149 --> 00:08:05.401
way back when I was in grad school,

00:08:05.401 --> 00:08:08.654
when Lenny Susskind and his collaborators
made the suggestion

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:13.910
that circuit complexity might be playing
a role in understanding quantum gravity.

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:15.578
And when you first hear

00:08:15.578 --> 00:08:19.499
this is a computer scientist,
this is a really provocative suggestion.

00:08:20.166 --> 00:08:23.586
Lenny had formulated
this, and I happened to be at that talk.

00:08:23.836 --> 00:08:27.340
And what I found hard to believe about it

00:08:27.548 --> 00:08:31.631
is that he was equating something
that is physical, you know, volume,

00:08:31.631 --> 00:08:36.432
with something that, as a complexity theorist, 
I would say, you know, this is quite

00:08:37.558 --> 00:08:40.269
un-physical: Quantum Circuit Complexity.

00:08:40.269 --> 00:08:43.606
That's something
that's manifestly hard to compute.

00:08:43.856 --> 00:08:47.193
I'm not sure we have particular
hypothesis, but something felt funny.

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:51.489
To put Susskind and Brown's Quantum
Circuit Complexity Theory of Black Holes

00:08:51.489 --> 00:08:52.406
to the test.

00:08:52.406 --> 00:08:56.202
Adam Bouland and his collaborators
brought in tools of modern cryptography

00:08:56.202 --> 00:08:57.870
to serve as a testing ground.

00:08:57.870 --> 00:09:00.998
The state that Susskind
and his collaborators were considering

00:09:01.207 --> 00:09:06.128
actually look a lot, like a quantum
analog of a block cipher.

00:09:06.337 --> 00:09:09.632
A block cipher is a type of algorithm
that underpins modern

00:09:09.632 --> 00:09:12.885
cryptography, including the technology
behind cryptocurrencies.

00:09:13.052 --> 00:09:16.180
By scrambling information
behind an encryption key-phrase

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:19.725
that must be entered exactly
right, to gain access to the information.

00:09:20.476 --> 00:09:22.728
For Adam Bouland,
the similarity to Susskind's

00:09:22.728 --> 00:09:25.731
theory was in the chaotic mixing
within a quantum system.

00:09:25.982 --> 00:09:29.944
His work drew a comparison between
scrambling the information in a cipher

00:09:30.069 --> 00:09:31.779
and the rearrangement of qubits,

00:09:31.779 --> 00:09:33.739
as a quantum system evolves...

00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:36.242
backing up Susskind's approach.

00:09:36.242 --> 00:09:41.455
Funilly, after this long
period of exploration, our work ended up

00:09:41.455 --> 00:09:46.419
in some roundabout way, justifying
Susskind and collaborators' conjectures.

00:09:46.502 --> 00:09:50.172
That was a really unexpected
result of our investigation.

00:09:51.132 --> 00:09:56.345
We realized that rather than reinforcing
our skepticism about his proposal,

00:09:56.345 --> 00:09:59.932
what our results suggested
was that, in fact,

00:10:00.224 --> 00:10:03.603
that was the only sensible way
to resolve the paradox.

00:10:04.061 --> 00:10:06.939
So that was a
that was a really interesting thing,

00:10:06.939 --> 00:10:10.276
you know, that
that he had this deep intuition.

00:10:10.651 --> 00:10:12.194
It's pretty remarkable!

00:10:15.281 --> 00:10:17.883
Faced with a possible explanation for life
after heat death,

00:10:17.883 --> 00:10:23.289
Susskind and his collaborators decided to propose
a new fundamental law of the universe.

00:10:23.831 --> 00:10:26.292
Eventually,
we just said, let's put forward the idea

00:10:26.292 --> 00:10:28.711
that computer scientists
had never put it forward...

00:10:28.711 --> 00:10:30.296
they hadn't said it.

00:10:30.296 --> 00:10:32.673
Let's put forward
the idea that there is a second

00:10:32.673 --> 00:10:35.676
law of complexity.

00:10:35.885 --> 00:10:38.888
That complexity behaves
very much like entropy,

00:10:39.096 --> 00:10:42.224
and all the ways on the average increases

00:10:42.683 --> 00:10:45.436
until it maxes out.

00:10:45.436 --> 00:10:47.772
We proposed
the second law of quantum complexity

00:10:47.772 --> 00:10:51.359
in direct analogy
to the second law of thermodynamics.

00:10:51.525 --> 00:10:56.364
I would say that it is not a law
in the sense that it's been proved.

00:10:56.364 --> 00:10:59.533
It is a conjecture that there should exist
such a law.

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:02.995
But the analogy with the classic second
law of thermodynamics

00:11:03.287 --> 00:11:08.584
is sufficiently strong that we felt
emboldened to propose such a law.

00:11:08.918 --> 00:11:13.589
At the moment, I think it's something
which really applies to black holes.

00:11:13.881 --> 00:11:18.219
The extent to which it applies to the
universe as a whole is not entirely clear.

00:11:18.636 --> 00:11:23.432
So this idea of using quantum
circuit complexity,

00:11:23.432 --> 00:11:26.977
this is treating quantum system
in a quantum mechanical way.

00:11:27.103 --> 00:11:30.189
Rather than starting
from a classical starting point

00:11:30.564 --> 00:11:33.567
into some semi classical approximation and

00:11:33.609 --> 00:11:35.911
and then bootstrap from there,

00:11:35.911 --> 00:11:41.534
that can take us somewhere, but it's not all the way.

00:11:41.617 --> 00:11:46.831
So once we accept that the world
is quantum and we fully embrace that,

00:11:46.997 --> 00:11:51.043
then we can actually understand things
much, much better

00:11:51.043 --> 00:11:54.880
because we're now
standing inside the quantum world

00:11:55.172 --> 00:11:58.259
and we're using fundamentally
quantum language

00:11:58.426 --> 00:12:01.637
to describe it
as we should have for a long time.

00:12:01.637 --> 00:12:04.932
And that will be a fundamental change

00:12:05.224 --> 00:12:08.227
of how
we think about these kind of systems.

00:12:08.436 --> 00:12:12.754
What the implications of complexity growth
are

00:12:12.754 --> 00:12:15.487
for the evolution of the universe.

00:12:15.487 --> 00:12:18.571
At the moment,
this is unexplored territory.

00:12:22.158 --> 00:12:25.286
Eventually,
if you wait an exponentially long time,

00:12:25.286 --> 00:12:28.330
even the quantum complexity achieves
its maximum value.

00:12:28.664 --> 00:12:33.252
Even the quantum complexity,
which is its own analog of heat death....

00:12:35.421 --> 00:12:38.424
and then maybe the story begins again.

00:12:41.469 --> 00:12:43.846
You know, as people say, if you wait

00:12:43.846 --> 00:12:48.017
long enough, everything will happen,
including what you started with.

